# .worktree-isolate — this repo OPTS IN to always-isolate session launching.
#
# Presence of this file is the whole contract. `~/.zshrc:_cc_route_check()` — the gate shared by
# every launcher (`claude`, `claude-default`, `claude-previous`) — reads it and, when a session
# starts in this repo's PRIMARY checkout, creates a per-session git worktree via
# `scripts/new-worktree.sh` and launches there instead of in the shared root.
#
# WHY A MARKER FILE and not a hardcoded repo name: the gate previously read
#   [[ "$(basename "$_top")" == "reso-management-app" ]]
# so isolation was a property of ONE name baked into an untracked 41 KB shell file. Every other
# repo silently launched in its shared checkout, and nothing in any repo could tell you that.
# The policy now lives in the repo it governs, in git, next to the script that implements it.
#
# WHY NOT `.harnessrc` (which docs/research/worktree-wrapper-pattern-2026-07-24.md §7.4 proposed):
# that name belongs to the installed `worktree-harness` tool, and we deliberately do NOT use it
# here — its defaults are silently wrong for this repo (worktree home `~/.worktrees` vs this
# repo's `~/Development/.worktrees`; an `npm ci` inferred from a package.json that exists only to
# render diagrams). Naming the marker `.harnessrc` would have implied a tool that is not in the
# path and invited someone to "fix" the mismatch by adopting it.
#
# WHAT THIS BUYS (docs/research/worktree-wrapper-pattern-2026-07-24.md §7.3): the shared checkout
# stops being asked to be two incompatible things at once — a scratch tree many sessions edit AND
# the clean ff-merge target `deploy-live.sh` needs. Under isolation no session edits root, so root
# stays clean and the deploy lane stays unblocked.
#
# TO OPT OUT for one launch: CLAUDE_ISOLATION_SKIP=1 claude
# The desk sets exactly that on its own respawns (scripts/desk-invariant.sh) — it is long-lived,
# machine-wide, and must stay pinned to a stable cwd rather than minting a worktree per respawn.
